# A.11 Unicode
E.append((r'''Any output the profile writes into a file or a page uses `\n`.''',
r'''Any output the profile writes into a file or a page uses `\n`.

**A.11 Unicode classes used by the rules above.** "Whitespace" is the Unicode White_Space property (it includes U+0085, U+00A0, U+1680, U+2000 to U+200A, U+2028, U+2029, U+202F, U+205F and U+3000, and excludes U+FEFF and U+200B); in Go, `unicode.IsSpace` has the same set. "Letter or digit" (the `alphanumeric` class of 5.1) is the Unicode Alphabetic property (letters, letter numbers and Other_Alphabetic marks) plus Numeric (Nd, Nl, No); in Go that is `unicode.IsLetter(r) || unicode.IsNumber(r) || unicode.Is(unicode.Nl, r) || unicode.Is(unicode.Other_Alphabetic, r)`. A combining accent that is not Other_Alphabetic (for example U+0301 in decomposed text) is a separator, so decomposed `não` splits into `na` and `o`; precomposed text is the normal case. "Lowercase" is the full Unicode lowercase mapping with the final-sigma rule (U+0130 lowercases to `i` followed by U+0307, and a word-final capital sigma to `ς`); Go's `strings.ToLower` maps U+0130 to a single `i` and has no final-sigma rule, so the two cases need explicit handling. "Characters" in length limits are Unicode scalar values; "bytes" are UTF-8 bytes; every cut falls on a scalar boundary. Language tags and path slugs are ASCII only.'''))

apply(P, E)
